Q: Finding an operator for 3 distinct values with bitwise operations A: <p>In complement to Sebastian Redl's answer, it is possible to follow a SWAR approach and vectorize up to 32 operators within a <code>std::uint64_t</code> register. The operator becomes:</p>
<pre><code>template&lt;typename T=std::uint64_t&gt;
auto op (T x, T y)  {
    T a_bit  = (x &amp; y) &amp; 0x5555555555555555;
    T a_mask = (a_bit &lt;&lt; 1) | a_bit;
    T b_or_c = (x ^ y) | 0x5555555555555555;
    return b_or_c &amp; a_mask;
}
</code></pre>
<p>One can check the whole thing as follows:</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;print&gt;
#include &lt;map&gt;
#include &lt;random&gt;
#include &lt;utility&gt;

using type = std::uint64_t;

constexpr type A=0b00;
constexpr type B=0b01;
constexpr type C=0b11;

template&lt;typename T=type&gt;
auto op (T x, T y)  {
    T a_bit  = (x &amp; y) &amp; 0x5555555555555555;
    T a_mask = (a_bit &lt;&lt; 1) | a_bit;
    T b_or_c = (x ^ y) | 0x5555555555555555;
    return b_or_c &amp; a_mask;
}

int main()
{
    std::map &lt;std::pair&lt;type,type&gt;, type&gt; truth = {
        {{A,A},A}, {{A,B},A}, {{A,C},A},
        {{B,A},A}, {{B,B},B}, {{B,C},C},
        {{C,A},A}, {{C,B},C}, {{C,C},B}
    };

    std::random_device rd;
    std::mt19937 gen(rd());
    std::uniform_int_distribution&lt;&gt; dis(0, 2);

    auto rdm = [&amp;] {
        auto r = dis(gen);
             if (r==0) { return A; }
        else if (r==1) { return B; }
        else if (r==2) { return C; }
        std::unreachable();
    };

    type x={};
    type y={};
    for (std::size_t i=0; i&lt;32; i++) {
        x = (x&lt;&lt;2) | rdm();
        y = (y&lt;&lt;2) | rdm();
    }

    // we compute 32 operators at the same time
    type z = op(x,y);

    // we check that each individual result is ok
    for (std::size_t i=0; i&lt;32; i++) {
        auto [xx,yy,zz] = std::make_tuple (x&amp;0b11, y&amp;0b11, z&amp;0b11);
        std::println (&quot;{:02b} op {:02b} = {:02b}  truth: {:02b}  isok: {}&quot;,
            xx, yy, zz, truth[std::make_pair(xx,yy)], zz==truth[std::make_pair(xx,yy)]
        );
        x &gt;&gt;= 2;
        y &gt;&gt;= 2;
        z &gt;&gt;= 2;
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://godbolt.org/z/W3dKG4331" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Demo</a></p>
<p>This scheme should be easily extended to SIMD (see <a href="https://godbolt.org/z/sz9z47TTY" rel="nofollow noreferrer">here</a>).</p>

Q: How to map JDBI SQL query result to Map&lt;Long, List&lt;Interest&gt;&gt; using annotations? A: <p>I think the normal pattern would be</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>public interface InterestDao {
   @SqlQuery(...)
   List&lt;Interest&gt; findInterests(@BindList Set&lt;Long&gt; eventIds);
}
</code></pre>
<p>Then convert to map via</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>import static java.util.stream.Collectors.*;
import static java.util.function.Function.*;

public class InterestService {
   private final InterestDao dao;

   // TODO constructor

   public Map&lt;Long, Interest&gt; findInterestsByEventIds(Set&lt;Long&gt; eventIds) {
      List&lt;Interest&gt; list = dao.findInterests(eventIds);
      return list.stream().collect(toMap(Interest::getInterestId, identity()));
   }
}
</code></pre>

Q: Vigenere cypher, incorrect code A: <p>Since it's the holiday season, I thought I would go ahead and analyze this code to highlight some of the issue causing problems for your program to execute a proper cipher.</p>
<p>First off, within the &quot;for&quot; loop that starts the encryption, the upper limit is being set to the length of the key and not the length of the string that was requested for input.</p>
<pre><code>int y = strlen(key);

for (int i=0; i &lt; y; i++){
</code></pre>
<p>So if the key is shorter than the entered string, the encrypted output will be truncated as in the following terminal output.</p>
<pre><code>craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/vig_c/bin/Release$ ./vig_c bc
Enter a string: ABCDE
HI
</code></pre>
<p>Alternatively, if the key would happen to be longer than the entered string, undefined behavior would occur.  Possibly, the encryption would happen as designed, but also one might see random characters displayed.  So, the length test needs to be corrected for the &quot;for&quot; loop.</p>
<p>Next, the upper and lowercase tests assume that if a string is entered in uppercase letters, the key is also in uppercase letters, and if the string is entered in lowercase letters, the key is also in lowercase letters.  This does not allow for the possible mixed cases within the text string or in the key, giving undesired encryption results as noted in the following terminal output examples.</p>
<pre><code>craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/vig_c/bin/Release$ ./vig_c bc
Enter a string: ABCDE ABCDE
HIJKL HIJKL

craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/vig_c/bin/Release$ ./vig_c bc
Enter a string: abcde abcde
bcdef bcdef

craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/vig_c/bin/Release$ ./vig_c Bc
Enter a string: abcde abcde
\]^_` \]^_`

craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/vig_c/bin/Release$ ./vig_c bc
Enter a string: AbCdE aBcDe
HcJeL bIdKf
</code></pre>
<p>And one last test using the encryption code and message from the Vigenere cipher web page does not yield the expected result.  Following is the expected result.</p>
<pre><code>craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/vig_c/bin/Release$ ./vig_c oculorhinolaryngology
Enter a string: attacking tonight
ovnlqbpvt hznzeuz
</code></pre>
<p>The actual output received was as follows.</p>
<pre><code>craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/vig_c/bin/Release$ ./vig_c oculorhinolaryngology
Enter a string: attacking tonight
o��oqyw|u �}|wuv�
</code></pre>
<p>As noted in the good comments, one of the issues is that the offset value will be fixed as the calculation &quot;x % y&quot; never changes.</p>
<p>With all of that considered, following is a refactored version of the code.</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;
#include &lt;string.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdbool.h&gt;
#include &lt;ctype.h&gt;

#define ALPHA 26                                /* Minimizing use of hard-coded values                                      */
#define SIZE 100

int main(int argc, char * argv[])               /* Using conventional character array pointer in lieu of CS50 string        */
{
    char key[ALPHA], s[SIZE];
    int counter = 0, cipher, x;

    if (argc != 2)
    {
        printf(&quot;error1\n&quot;);
        return 1;
    }

    strcpy(key, argv[1]);

    for (int i=0; i &lt; strlen(key); i++)
    {
        if (((isalpha(key[i])) == false))
        {
            printf(&quot;error2\n&quot;);
            return 2;
        }
    }

    printf(&quot;Enter a string: &quot;);
    if (fgets(s, SIZE - 1, stdin) == NULL)      /* Using conventional string input function in lieu of CS50 get_string()    */
        return 3;

    x = strlen(key);

    for (int i = 0; i &lt; x; i++)
    {
        key[i] = tolower(key[i]) - 'a';         /* Convert key character into its equivalent offset value                   */
    }

    for (int i=0; i &lt; strlen(s); i++)
    {
        cipher = s[i];                          /* Store character into cipher variable up front to streamline              */

        if (islower(s[i]))
        {
            cipher = s[i] + key[counter % x];
            if (cipher &gt; 'z')
                cipher -= ALPHA;
            counter++;
        }
        else if (isupper(s[i]))
        {
            cipher = s[i] + key[counter % x];
            if (cipher &gt; 'Z')
                cipher -= ALPHA;
            counter++;
        }

        printf(&quot;%c&quot;, cipher);
    }

    printf(&quot;\n&quot;);
    return 0;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Following are the main things to take away from the refactoring.</p>
<ul>
<li><p>Since I don't have the Harvard CS50 library installed on my virtual machine, standard variable types and standard functions were used in lieu of the additional types within the CS50 library (e.g. &quot;char *&quot; in lieu of &quot;string&quot; and &quot;fgets()&quot; in lieu of &quot;get_string()&quot;.  This is just a minor change that did not affect the code functionality.</p>
</li>
<li><p>In lieu of hard-coded numbers, #define directives were used to mitigate the use of what is sometimes called &quot;magic numbers&quot; to clarify the functionality within the program.</p>
</li>
<li><p>To minimize whether or not the key or message string have uppercase, lowercase, or mixed cases, the character array is updated to store the desired offset value for the characters entered in the key.</p>
</li>
<li><p>To ensure that the incrementation of the key only occurred for characters within a character array were being encrypted, a separate counter variable was used.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>With those bits of refactoring, following is the terminal output of a couple of tests.</p>
<pre><code>craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/vig_c/bin/Release$ ./vig_c bc
Enter a string: ABCDE ABCDE
BDDFF CCEEG

craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/vig_c/bin/Release$ ./vig_c oculorhinolaryngology
Enter a string: attacking tonight
ovnlqbpvt hznzeuz
</code></pre>
<p>From reviewing this issue and code, the main takeaways would be to probably delve more into usage of character arrays, including attributes such as string lengths, defining constant values via the &quot;#define&quot; directive, and usage of the modulo (%) function.</p>
